RayCAD patch to OCCT 8.0.1 (LGPL-2.1 with the OCCT exception; this file is under the same terms).

What: a cancel reaches the slow parts of BOPAlgo_RemoveFeatures (BRepAlgoAPI_Defeaturing).
  - FillGap::TrimExtendedFaces passes each face's share of its progress range to FillGap::TrimFace, which passes it
    to the General Fuse that trims the extended face (aGFTrim.Perform). Before, that General Fuse ran without a range,
    so its BOPAlgo_PaveFiller never saw a user break.
  - BOPAlgo_EdgeFace passes its job's range to IntTools_EdgeFace::Perform, which passes it to
    IntTools_BeanFaceIntersector::Perform (both take an optional Message_ProgressRange now); the bean/face
    intersector polls it between the steps of its localized and extremum searches and stops, not done, on a break.
    Before, one edge/face job ran to its end however long it took.

Why: RayCAD runs direct edits on a kernel thread it abandons on cancel, and requires the abandoned OCCT work to stop
within 10 s (task T-080). Removing fillets of the NIST CTC 05 and CTC 02 test parts (tests/unit/kernel,
directedit_corpus_test.cpp, the S9 set) spent minutes trimming extended B-spline faces: one went on for 237 s after a
cancel, sampled with gdb inside FillGap::TrimFace > BOPAlgo_PaveFiller::PerformEF > IntTools_EdgeFace >
IntTools_BeanFaceIntersector::ComputeLocalized > Extrema_GenExtCS. With the first change it stopped 16 s after the
cancel, at the end of the edge/face job it was in; the second bounds that by one extremum search. A broken job's
partial result is never used: BOPAlgo_PaveFiller::PerformEF checks the break after its jobs and the removal ends with
BOPAlgo_AlertUserBreak, as it already did when the break was seen elsewhere. A run that is not cancelled is unchanged
(the ranges only report progress and answer the break).

Upstream: not reported.

